.TH FBMQ-DESIGN 7 "February 2026" "fbmq 1.0.0" "Miscellaneous"
.SH NAME
fbmq-design \- architecture and design rationale
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fbmq
is a file-based message queue. Each queue is a directory. Each message is a
Markdown file. Atomic
.BR rename (2)
is the sole coordination primitive.
.SH DIRECTORY LAYOUT
.nf
<queue>/
    pending/            Flat directory (or with priority subdirs)
    processing/         Claimed (timestamp-prefixed)
    done/               Completed
    failed/             Dead-letter
    .tmp/               Atomic write staging
    .meta/              Queue configuration (max_pending)
.fi
.SH IDENTITY
Message ID = 128 bits of random entropy formatted as 32-char hex.
Entropy from arc4random_buf(3) on macOS, getrandom(2) on Linux,
with /dev/urandom fallback.
.SH CONCURRENCY
Lock-free critical path.
.BR rename (2)
is atomic \(em one consumer wins, others get ENOENT.
Depth is computed by scanning pending/ (or 4 priority subdirs) plus
processing/ on each call.
.SH MAX PENDING
A configurable limit on the number of pending messages (default: 10000).
Set at init time with \fB--max-pending N\fR and stored in
\fI.meta/max_pending\fR. Enqueue returns ENOSPC when the limit is reached.
Use \fB--max-pending 0\fR for unlimited.
.PP
The limit is a \fIsoft limit\fR: under high concurrency, two producers may
both pass the check before either completes its rename, briefly exceeding
the cap. Fixing this TOCTOU window would require file locking, which
contradicts the lock-free rename(2)-only design. The overshoot is bounded
by the number of concurrent producers and is acceptable in practice.
.SH DEQUEUE CONTENTION
Pop collects all pending .md entries, sorts them by filename (which embeds
a fixed-width enqueue timestamp), and attempts to claim the oldest via
.BR rename (2).
If another consumer claimed it first, the rename fails with ENOENT and
pop falls through to the next-oldest entry. If all entries are raced away,
the scan retries (up to \fBFBMQ_SCAN_RETRIES\fR times).
.PP
A single consumer gets strict FIFO ordering. Under multi-consumer
contention, all consumers attempt the oldest message first \(em one wins
the rename, others fall through to next-oldest. This provides best-effort
FIFO: the oldest message is always attempted first, but scheduling
determines which consumer gets it. The sort costs O(n\ log\ n) per pop,
negligible for typical queue sizes.
.SH RELIABILITY
At-least-once delivery. Lease-based reaping. Dead-letter after max retries.
TTL expiry for time-bounded messages.
.SH NACK CRASH WINDOW
The \fBnack\fR operation is not atomic. It creates a new file in the
destination directory (pending/ or failed/) via rename from .tmp/, then
unlinks the original claimed file from processing/. If the process crashes
between these two steps, the message exists in both locations simultaneously.
.PP
Running \fBfbmq reap\fR periodically (e.g. via cron) is required for crash
safety. The reaper detects messages in processing/ whose corresponding
file already exists in pending/ or failed/, and removes the stale
processing/ copy. Until the reaper runs, such messages may be delivered
twice (at\-least\-once semantics).
.SH PERFORMANCE
.TS
l l l.
Mode	Throughput	Durability
_
tmpfs, --no-fsync	100K+ msg/s	None
SSD, batched fsync	30-50K msg/s	Per batch
SSD, per-msg fsync	200-500 msg/s	Full
.TE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fbmq (1),
.BR fbmq-message (5)
